/** \page boundary_bridge_docu Closing two boundary loops with an optimal strip

\section boundary_bridge_intro Introduction

OpenMesh::Utils::bridge_boundaries() connects two complete boundary loops of
a triangle mesh with a triangle strip chosen by dynamic programming.  It lives
in <tt>OpenMesh/Tools/Utils/BoundaryBridgeT.hh</tt> and is a header-only
template, so it works with any OpenMesh mesh type whose faces are triangles
(for example OpenMesh::TriMesh_ArrayKernelT<>).

The function never splits edges, never welds vertices and never touches
vertices or existing faces beyond recomputing already requested normals.  All
existing handles and point positions remain valid.

\section boundary_bridge_input Input contract

Both input vectors must list one complete boundary loop each, following the
<tt>next</tt> pointers of the loop's boundary (faceless) halfedges.  The first
vertex is not repeated at the end.  The edge between the two first vertices is
the designated seam.  The loops must not share a vertex, must each contain
distinct vertices, and may have between 3 and 32 vertices.  All coordinates
must be finite.  \c lambda must be finite and non-negative.

Writing <tt>a = first</tt> and <tt>b</tt> for \c second traversed backwards
from its first vertex, a legal strip is a monotone lattice path from (0,0) to
(n,m).  A right step at (i,j) adds the triangle (a_i, a_{i+1}, b_j), an up
step adds (a_i, b_{j+1}, b_j), with indices taken modulo the loop lengths.  A
cross-loop vertex pair may be used only once, except for the seam pair which
closes the strip.  Every new triangle must have area greater than 1e-12, and
pre-existing cross-loop edges may not be reused.

The selected strip minimises

\f[
  \sum_{\text{new }f} \operatorname{area}(f)
  + \lambda \sum_{(f,g)\text{ adjacent}} \lVert n_f - n_g \rVert^2,
\f]

where the adjacency is cyclic: the first and last new triangle contribute one
term as well.  The \c lambda term is therefore not dropped at the closure.
Ties smaller than 1e-9 (relative) are acceptable.

\section boundary_bridge_uv Texture coordinates

On success the standard halfedge attribute <tt>h:texcoords2D</tt> is
allocated if necessary and filled for all new faces.  Let S be the normalised
arc length accumulated along each input loop from the seam vertex, with S = 0
on the first loop and S = 1 on the second loop.  A halfedge texture coordinate
belongs to the corner at its to-vertex, so the seam vertex receives S = 0 in
the first triangle and S = 1 in the last triangle, where the same geometric
vertex appears on both sides of the strip.

\section boundary_bridge_persist OM persistence

The OM binary format stores the standard halfedge texture coordinates in an
anonymous Halfedge/Texcoord chunk, written only when
OpenMesh::IO::Options::FaceTexCoord is requested and the attribute exists.  The
chunk lists one Vec2f per face corner in file face order and per-face vertex
order.  The remaining entries up to 2E are padded with zeros, so readers can
consume the chunk sequentially; boundary halfedges without a face are not
meaningful for rendering and need not be stored.  The reader assigns values
corner by corner while reconstructing the faces, so it does not rely on
halfedge slot numbers being stable between writing and reading.  If
FaceTexCoord is not requested, the chunk is skipped without disturbing later
chunks.  Files without the chunk remain readable.

\section boundary_bridge_example Example

\code
#include <OpenMesh/Core/Mesh/TriMesh_ArrayKernelT.hh>
#include <OpenMesh/Tools/Utils/BoundaryBridgeT.hh>

typedef OpenMesh::TriMesh_ArrayKernelT<>  MyMesh;

// ... fill "mesh" so that "first" and "second" are complete boundary loops
//     listed in boundary next direction.

OpenMesh::Utils::BoundaryBridgeResultT<MyMesh> result;
OpenMesh::Utils::bridge_boundaries(mesh, first, second, 1.0, result);

std::cout << result.faces.size() << " new triangles, energy "
          << result.energy << std::endl;
\endcode

A complete, buildable example is provided in
<tt>src/OpenMesh/Examples/boundary_bridge.cc</tt>.
*/

